WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.150
Linguistics isn’t just the study of
words or sounds:

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.220
it’s the study of all the parts of conversation.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:08.450
That includes gesture analysis, which looks
at “co-speech gestures”:

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.139
not posture or gaze,
but winking, or sticking out your tongue,

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:14.280
or how arms and hands move alongside speech.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.360
Just to be clear, this isn’t about
sign languages:

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.220
those are systematic and rule-governed with
elaborate syntax and vocabulary.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.140
Signed languages are language, but gestures
are “paralinguistic”.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.160
They accompany language.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.300
Gesture analysts make their job easier by
talking about different categories of gestures,

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.180
which explain how they relate to meaning.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.580
And those are:

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:34.980
Number one: Iconic.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.840
These gestures represent a literal object,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.270
such as a flat surface, or
a car weaving through traffic.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.370
Number two: Metaphoric.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.360
Gestures that symbolize an abstract concept,

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.030
like “before” and “after”,
or “working together”.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.110
I do this a lot, and on this greenscreen those
metaphoric gestures

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.850
become a bit more literal with the help of
graphics.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.530
Apologies to my animator.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:54.530
Three: Deictic.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.070
That’s pointing to things,

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.440
positioning yourself to people,
or places or things,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.000
like pointing behind the camera
or ‘over there’.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.600
Number four: Pragmatic.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.990
Like offering the floor to someone,

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.130
or "don't bother me right now".

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.020
And finally, Beat.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.470
The rhythm of gestures alongside the
natural stress patterns of speech.

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:13.760
All these categories--

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.360
I’m really self-conscious about every move
I’m making now.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.570
These categories can coexist as well:

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.900
often gestures that are performed ‘on beat’
also fit into another category.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.510
There’s one more type of gesture that serves
a slightly different purpose: emblems.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:32.100
Emblems are named gestures that don’t have
to occur alongside speech to have meaning.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.100
Like “thumbs up”.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.550
It has a meaning outside of
the context of speech:

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.800
in a lot of the world, you can flash someone
the thumbs up

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.200
and they'll almost certainly understand that
you're signalling approval,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.940
or that everything's okay.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.740
But if you swap the fingers,
it doesn’t have the same meaning.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:47.850
And even If I stick with the thumb,

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.630
if I change angle and arm position,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.950
I’m trying to hitch-hike.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.479
The form of the gesture is tied
to the established meaning.

00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:56.520
It’s an emblem.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.680
Which means it’s not universal.
The meaning is learned.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.670
And context can change it.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:01.670
That thumbs up?

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:02.979
In some countries, it used to be rude,

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:06.410
but because of globalization, that’s no
longer the common reading there.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:09.080
Although there will be people who remember its
rude history

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.400
and could still interpret it
the way.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.320
Emblems can change definition over time,
they can be created, they can be forgotten.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.500
I would demonstrate that by dabbing,

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.590
but I don’t want a GIF of that to
haunt me for the rest of my life.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.840
So why do we do it?

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.820
What does gesturing actually accomplish?

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.460
Well, first: redundancy for
decoding and encoding errors.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:32.480
Languages have agreement all over the place:
there’s redundancy baked into everything.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.709
Spoken language is ephemeral.

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:36.459
Once something is said, it's gone.

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:40.230
Those sound waves have moved on
and they are not coming back.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.460
Gesture is another form of redundancy:

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.750
it doesn't matter if a loud [engine noise]
drove by at just the wrong time–

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.640
you may have figured out a vague idea
of what was being said.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:51.650
Next, you can show emphasis and importance.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:53.480
Or if you’re explaining an abstract concept,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.080
using metaphoric gestures to visibly explain
things can help make it clear --

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.720
either to your conversation partner, or just
in your own head.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.020
And sometimes, it is so much easier
and clearer just to point.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.140
Gestures are important to communication,

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.720
and so important that if our
hands and arms are unavailable,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.660
we will use our head, our eyes,
our other limbs to compensate.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.840
Gestures can also show
understanding of social norms.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.050
If you’re embarrassed to say something,

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.110
you can make that clear by covering your face
or your eyes,

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:21.819
or by doing the move that looks like

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:24.360
"there are no paper towels or hand dryers
in this bathroom so

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.760
"I guess I'll just shake ’em dry".

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.700
Or if you want to distance yourself from what
you’re saying you can use scare quotes,

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.360
or you can put a boundary around yourself.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.020
Gestures also seem to help us
get the words out in the first place.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:40.160
That's why people gesture even when
they’re on the phone,

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.220
and why people who have been
blind since birth gesture.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.660
Gesture is intrinsic to language.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.270
It helps us communicate more effectively and
more elaborately,

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:54.120
and to pass on information and feelings that
are difficult to put into words.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.160
Gestures are basically emoji
for the real world.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.660
One of my co-authors, Gretchen McCulloch,
has a book called Because Internet,

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:03.640
all about internet language.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.420
You can find out more at the links
in the description.

